    "def": "A precis is a compressed restatement of a passage in your own words, keeping every essential idea and the author's line of argument, and discarding everything that merely illustrates or decorates it. It is a faithful miniature of the original, not a comment on it.",
    "exp": "The three things a precis is not are the three ways candidates lose marks. It is not a summary of the parts you found interesting: you must keep the author's emphasis, even where you disagree. It is not a review: no praise, no criticism, no 'the author rightly says'. And it is not an abridgement made by deletion: crossing out sentences and joining what is left is not a precis, because the result reads as fragments of someone else's prose.\n\nThe test of a good precis is that a reader who has never seen the original would come away with the same understanding of the argument, in less time. That means the logical connectives matter as much as the content. If the passage runs 'although X, nevertheless Y, and therefore Z', your precis must preserve that shape. Compression that turns an argument into a list of assertions has lost the thing worth keeping.\n\nWrite it as a single continuous paragraph unless the paper says otherwise. Do not use headings, bullet points or numbering. Do not quote. Do not address the reader. Give it a title.",
