    "def": "The standard expectation is that a precis runs to about one third of the original. Where the question paper states a word limit instead, that figure overrides the rule and must be obeyed within a narrow margin.",
    "exp": "Work the arithmetic before you write, not after. Count or estimate the passage length, divide by three, and write that number at the top of your rough sheet as a target. A 300-word passage wants roughly 100 words; a 450-word passage wants about 150. Treat plus or minus ten per cent as the safe band.\n\nEstimating length is faster than counting every word and accurate enough. Count the words in three full lines, take the average, and multiply by the number of lines. Do this for the original, and again for your own draft.\n\nDiscipline matters because the limit is examinable in itself. A precis that is too long has not done the work of compression, and one that is far too short has dropped ideas. Both lose marks even when the prose is good. Draft first, count second, then cut - cutting is easier and safer than padding, and a first draft that comes out a little long is a better starting point than one that is short.\n\nIf the paper gives a word count, write it in the margin and state your own count at the end. Many examiners expect it, and none penalise it.",
