    "def": "The controlling idea is the single claim the whole passage exists to advance. Everything else in the passage either supports it, qualifies it, or illustrates it. Identifying it correctly is the one decision that determines whether the precis succeeds.",
    "exp": "Read the passage twice. The first reading is for sense: do not underline anything, just follow the argument. The second is analytical, and it has one question - what is this passage trying to get me to accept?\n\nThe controlling idea is often stated outright, and when it is, it usually sits in the first or last sentence of the passage or of its opening paragraph. But it is not always stated. In a passage built entirely from examples, the controlling idea is the generalisation the examples add up to, and you will have to phrase it yourself.\n\nA reliable check: write the idea in one sentence, then take each paragraph in turn and ask what it does to that sentence. If every paragraph supports, qualifies or illustrates it, you have the right idea. If a paragraph seems to be doing something unrelated, either you have the wrong controlling idea or you have missed a turn in the argument - and a turn is exactly what a well-set passage hides.\n\nOnce you have it, write it first. The rest of the precis arranges itself around that sentence in the order the author used.",
