    "unitTitle": "Letter Writing",
    "sub": "Applications and representations",
    "def": "An application requests something the writer is entitled to ask for - leave, a post, a certificate. A representation asks an authority to reconsider or redress something the writer believes to be wrong. Both are submitted upward, and both are commonly set on this paper.",
    "exp": "An application is short and factual. State what is sought in the first sentence, give the grounds and the relevant dates, state the period where one applies, and close with the request. Leave applications are routed through the immediate superior, so the address block carries 'Through: The ...' naming that officer. Documents supporting the claim go in the enclosures.\n\nA representation is longer and must be careful in tone, because it disputes something. Set out the facts in date order first, without argument. Then state plainly what you believe is wrong and on what basis - a rule, an order, a precedent. Then state the specific relief sought. Do not editorialise, do not attribute motive, and do not threaten to escalate; a representation that reads as a complaint invites a refusal on tone alone.\n\nBoth forms open with the writer's standing: name, designation and office, so the recipient knows who is asking and in what capacity. Both close with the standard subscription, and both are signed with name and designation.\n\nThe single most useful habit in either is to make the ask unmistakable. An authority should be able to read the first and last sentences and know exactly what is wanted.",
