    "id": "c23",
    "paper": "TECH1",
    "unit": "I",
    "unitTitle": "Fundamentals of Computer",
    "sub": "CPU Architecture and Instruction Cycle",
    "def": "The CPU is built from an arithmetic-logic unit, a control unit and a set of registers, linked by internal buses. It runs a program by repeating the instruction cycle: fetch the next instruction from memory, decode it, execute it, write back the result, then check for interrupts and start again.",
    "exp": "The registers you must be able to name. The program counter holds the address of the next instruction. The memory address register holds the address currently being accessed and drives the address bus. The memory data or buffer register holds the data or instruction in transit on the data bus. The instruction register holds the instruction currently being decoded. The accumulator holds intermediate ALU results, alongside general-purpose registers and a flag or status register.\n\nThe cycle. In fetch, the address in the program counter is placed in the MAR, memory returns the instruction into the MDR and then into the instruction register, and the program counter is incremented. In decode, the control unit interprets the opcode and identifies the operands and addressing mode. In execute, the ALU performs the operation, or a data transfer or branch takes place. In writeback, the result is stored to a register or back to memory. The processor then tests for pending interrupts before the next fetch.\n\nOne instruction cycle may need several machine cycles (memory read, memory write, I/O), and each machine cycle takes one or more clock cycles.\n\nPipelining overlaps the stages so that one instruction executes while the next is decoded and a third is fetched. Modern processors add superscalar issue, multiple cores and on-chip cache. CISC designs such as x86 use many variable-length instructions; RISC designs such as ARM use few fixed-length ones.",
    "facts": [
      "The instruction cycle is fetch, decode, execute, with writeback and interrupt check commonly listed as further steps.",
      "The program counter holds the address of the next instruction; the instruction register holds the instruction currently being executed.",
      "MAR holds an address and connects to the address bus; MDR or MBR holds data and connects to the data bus.",
      "One instruction cycle contains one or more machine cycles, and each machine cycle takes one or more clock cycles.",
      "Pipelining increases instruction throughput; it does not shorten the execution time of any single instruction.",
      "RISC uses a small set of fixed-length instructions and relies on the compiler; CISC uses a large set of complex variable-length instructions."
    ],
    "traps": [
      "PC versus IR is the most-asked register question: the program counter holds the address of the NEXT instruction, the instruction register holds the CURRENT one.",
      "Instruction cycle, machine cycle and clock cycle are nested, not synonyms. An option equating them is wrong.",
      "Pipelining improves throughput, not the latency of one instruction. Options saying it \"makes each instruction faster\" are the standard distractor.",
      "Decoding is done by the control unit, not the ALU. The ALU only executes arithmetic and logic."
    ],
    "mnem": "FDE: Fetch, Decode, Execute.",
    "rel": [
      "Functional Components of a Computer",
      "Cache Memory",
      "Motherboard and Chipset"
    ]
