    "id": "c57",
    "paper": "TECH1",
    "unit": "I",
    "unitTitle": "Fundamentals of Computer",
    "sub": "Data Privacy and Personal Data Protection",
    "def": "Data privacy is an individual's right to control how information about them is collected, used, shared and retained; personal data protection is the legal and technical machinery that enforces that right. India's governing statute is the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023, which applies to digital personal data - data about an identifiable individual - and places duties on whoever decides why and how it is processed, including government departments.",
    "exp": "Learn the two roles first. The data principal is the individual the data is about; for a child, the parent or lawful guardian acts for them. The data fiduciary is the person or body that determines the purpose and means of processing. The word fiduciary is deliberate: the data is held in trust for the principal, not owned. A data processor only processes on a fiduciary's instructions, and organisations handling data at scale or higher risk can be notified as significant data fiduciaries with extra duties.\n\nConsent must be free, specific, informed, unconditional and unambiguous, given by a clear affirmative act, preceded by a plain-language notice, and withdrawable as easily as it was given. It is not the only lawful route - certain legitimate uses, including the State providing a subsidy, benefit, certificate or service, do not need fresh consent.\n\nThe operating principles are purpose limitation (use it only for the purpose stated), data minimisation (collect only what that purpose needs), storage limitation (erase once the purpose is served), accuracy, and reasonable security safeguards, with notification of a breach to the Board and to the affected individuals.\n\nThe data principal's rights are access to information about processing, correction and erasure, grievance redressal, and nomination of someone to exercise the rights on death or incapacity. The Data Protection Board of India adjudicates.",
    "facts": [
      "The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 is India's law on digital personal data",
      "Data principal = the individual the data is about; data fiduciary = whoever decides the purpose and means of processing",
      "Consent must be free, specific, informed, unconditional and unambiguous, and withdrawable as easily as given",
      "Purpose limitation, data minimisation and storage limitation are core obligations of a data fiduciary",
      "Rights of a data principal include access, correction, erasure, grievance redressal and nomination",
      "The Data Protection Board of India handles complaints and adjudicates on breaches"
    ],
    "traps": [
      "Data principal and data fiduciary reversed in the options - the principal is the human being, the fiduciary is the organisation holding the data",
      "Privacy is not security - security keeps data from unauthorised access; privacy limits even authorised staff to the purpose the data was collected for. Encrypted data used for a new purpose is still a privacy breach.",
      "The Act covers digital personal data about identifiable individuals - not company or business data, and not truly anonymised data",
      "Consent is not the only lawful basis - certain legitimate uses, notably the State delivering a subsidy, benefit or service, are permitted without fresh consent"
    ],
    "mnem": "",
    "rel": [
      "Cyber Ethics",
      "Electronic Records management",
      "Digital India and e-Governance"
    ]
