    "id": "c60",
    "paper": "TECH1",
    "unit": "I",
    "unitTitle": "Fundamentals of Computer",
    "sub": "Digital Inclusion and Accessibility",
    "def": "Digital inclusion is ensuring that everyone can actually use digital services, whatever their income, location, age, education, language or disability. Accessibility is the narrower and legally enforceable part of it: that a website, application or document can be used by persons with disabilities, working correctly with assistive technology such as screen readers.",
    "exp": "The digital divide has four axes, and connectivity is only the first. Availability - no reliable broadband or mobile coverage in remote hill and border areas. Affordability - the cost of a device and of data. Skills - a citizen who has a smartphone but cannot complete an online application. And language - a portal published only in English or Hindi excludes speakers of Mizo and other state languages, so local-language interfaces and content are an inclusion measure, not a courtesy. Assisted access through Common Service Centres is the standard mitigation where a citizen cannot transact alone.\n\nAccessibility has a defined standard: WCAG, the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines from the W3C, built on four principles - Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust - with conformance levels A, AA and AAA, of which AA is the usual target. In practice that means alternative text on images so a screen reader such as NVDA, JAWS or TalkBack can describe them, full operation from the keyboard without a mouse, sufficient colour contrast, captions and transcripts for audio and video, properly labelled form fields, and never conveying meaning by colour alone.\n\nIn India this is an obligation, not a preference. The Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016 requires government information and services, including websites, to be accessible, and GIGW is the compliance standard for government sites.",
    "facts": [
      "WCAG's four principles are Perceivable, Operable, Understandable and Robust, with conformance levels A, AA and AAA",
      "The Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016 requires government websites, information and services to be accessible",
      "GIGW, the Guidelines for Indian Government Websites, is the accessibility and quality standard for government sites and builds on WCAG",
      "NVDA, JAWS and Android TalkBack are screen readers; they depend on alternative text and correct heading structure",
      "The digital divide has availability, affordability, skill and language dimensions, not connectivity alone",
      "Common Service Centres provide assisted access for citizens who cannot complete a transaction themselves"
    ],
    "traps": [
      "Accessibility vs usability - usability is ease of use for everyone; accessibility is specifically use by persons with disabilities, and for government sites it is a statutory duty",
      "Access is not inclusion - providing connectivity achieves nothing if the citizen lacks the skill or cannot read the interface language",
      "A scanned PDF is an image and a screen reader cannot read it; an accessible document needs real, tagged text",
      "Alt text is not a caption - alt text describes an image for users who cannot see it, captions transcribe audio for users who cannot hear it"
    ],
    "mnem": "POUR - Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust",
    "rel": [
      "Digital India and e-Governance",
      "Cyber Ethics",
      "Digital Transformation and e-Governance"
    ]
