    "id": "c67",
    "paper": "TECH2",
    "unit": "I",
    "unitTitle": "Computer Networking",
    "sub": "Routing and Switching",
    "def": "Switching moves frames within a single network using MAC addresses, at OSI layer 2. Routing moves packets between different networks using IP addresses, at OSI layer 3. A switch consults a MAC address table; a router consults a routing table. The layer and the address type are what separate the two operations.",
    "exp": "A layer-2 switch learns the source MAC of every frame it receives and records it against the port it arrived on. When that MAC later appears as a destination, the switch forwards the frame out only that one port. A frame addressed to an unknown MAC, or a broadcast, is flooded out every other port. A switching decision never inspects the IP header.\n\nA router reads the destination IP address, picks the longest matching prefix in its routing table, decrements the TTL, rebuilds the layer-2 frame and forwards the packet to the next hop. Routers stop broadcasts, so each router interface bounds a separate broadcast domain. A host sends anything outside its own subnet to its default gateway, which is a router interface.\n\nRoutes are either static, typed in by the administrator, or dynamic, learned from a routing protocol. Distance-vector protocols such as RIP pick the path with the fewest hops and RIP will not accept a route beyond 15 hops. Link-state protocols such as OSPF build a map of the area and run Dijkstra's shortest-path algorithm on a cost derived from bandwidth. BGP is the path-vector protocol that routes between autonomous systems and holds the Internet together.\n\nLayer-3 switches blur the boundary: they switch inside VLANs at wire speed and route between them.",
    "facts": [
      "Switching is layer 2 on MAC addresses; routing is layer 3 on IP addresses.",
      "A switch floods a frame out all other ports when the destination MAC is unknown or the frame is a broadcast.",
      "Routers separate broadcast domains; a plain switch does not unless VLANs are configured.",
      "RIP is distance-vector with a 15-hop maximum; OSPF is link-state and uses Dijkstra's algorithm; BGP routes between autonomous systems.",
      "A router forwards using the longest-prefix match in its routing table and decrements the TTL on each hop.",
      "A host sends any packet destined outside its own subnet to its default gateway."
    ],
    "traps": [
      "Routing vs switching is decided by the OSI layer and the address type, not by speed or by the size of the box.",
      "Switches break up collision domains but not broadcast domains. Only a router or a VLAN breaks a broadcast domain.",
      "RIP's metric is hop count and ignores link speed, so it can prefer a slow two-hop path over a fast three-hop one. OSPF, costing on bandwidth, would not.",
      "A router with no matching route and no default route drops the packet - it does not flood it the way a switch floods an unknown frame."
    ],
    "mnem": "Switch means MAC and local; route means IP and remote.",
    "rel": [
      "Switches",
      "Routers",
      "OSI Model"
    ]
