    "id": "c68",
    "paper": "TECH2",
    "unit": "I",
    "unitTitle": "Computer Networking",
    "sub": "Hubs",
    "def": "A hub is a layer-1 (Physical layer) device that joins several Ethernet devices into one segment. It is a multiport repeater: it regenerates the incoming signal and pushes it out of every other port, with no knowledge of addresses. Every frame reaching a hub is delivered to every attached device - a hub transfers a message by broadcasting it.",
    "exp": "Because a hub only repeats bits, it makes no forwarding decision at all. If host A sends a frame to host B, every host on the hub receives a copy; only B's NIC accepts it and the others discard it. That wastes bandwidth and is trivially insecure, since any machine set to promiscuous mode can read all the traffic on the segment.\n\nEvery port on a hub belongs to one collision domain and one broadcast domain. Two hosts transmitting at the same instant collide, both back off, and throughput collapses as more hosts are added. A hub is therefore half-duplex, and its bandwidth is shared rather than per-port: a 10 Mbps hub with ten busy hosts gives each about 1 Mbps.\n\nHubs come in three named forms. A passive hub only joins the wires and does not regenerate the signal. An active hub amplifies and regenerates the signal, and is the ordinary Ethernet hub. An intelligent or manageable hub adds monitoring and management on top of that.\n\nHubs are obsolete in practice - switches cost about the same and are strictly better, so no current network is built with them. They survive on exam papers as the canonical layer-1 device and the canonical broadcast-to-all-ports device.",
    "facts": [
      "A hub operates at OSI layer 1, the Physical layer, and is defined as a multiport repeater.",
      "A hub forwards an incoming frame to all other ports by broadcast; it does not read MAC addresses.",
      "All ports of a hub form a single collision domain and a single broadcast domain.",
      "Hub bandwidth is shared among all connected hosts, and hub operation is half-duplex.",
      "Hub types: passive (no regeneration), active (regenerates the signal), intelligent (adds management).",
      "A hub has no MAC address table, so it cannot filter or segment traffic."
    ],
    "traps": [
      "'Which device transfers a message by broadcasting it to every port?' is the hub, not the switch. A switch sends a known unicast to one port only.",
      "A hub does not break up collision domains. A switch gives every port its own collision domain; a hub gives all ports one shared domain.",
      "Layer assignment is the standard distractor: hub is layer 1, switch is layer 2, router is layer 3.",
      "A hub is not a 'cheap switch'. It cannot learn, filter, or run full duplex, so adding hosts to a hub degrades everyone's throughput."
    ],
    "mnem": "Hub broadcasts, switch selects.",
    "rel": [
      "Switches",
      "Types of Networks",
      "Routing and Switching"
    ]
