    "id": "c71",
    "paper": "TECH2",
    "unit": "I",
    "unitTitle": "Computer Networking",
    "sub": "Gateways",
    "def": "A gateway is a device or software that joins two networks using different protocols, data formats or architectures, translating traffic so each side understands the other. Unlike a router, which forwards IP packets between IP networks that already speak the same protocol, a gateway converts. It can operate at any layer and often works right up at the application layer.",
    "exp": "The clearest examples are the ones that clearly convert something. A VoIP gateway converts between IP telephony and the analogue or digital circuits of the public telephone network. An email gateway takes mail from an internal messaging system and re-formats it as SMTP for the internet. A payment gateway sits between a merchant application and the banking network, translating a web transaction into the format the bank switch accepts. An IoT gateway collects readings from sensors on a short-range wireless protocol and forwards them over IP.\n\nThe confusion the exam exploits comes from the term default gateway. Your PC's default gateway is the IP address it sends packets to when the destination is outside its own subnet — and on almost every LAN that address belongs to a router, not to a protocol translator. So in everyday and home usage every router is loosely called a gateway. Both statements can be true in the same question: the default gateway is a router doing routing; a gateway in the strict sense is doing translation.\n\nPractically, a gateway is slower than a router for the same traffic because translation costs processing, and it is usually a single point of failure — if the gateway is down, the two systems it joins cannot talk at all.",
    "facts": [
      "A gateway translates between different protocols or architectures; a router forwards between IP networks using the same protocol.",
      "A gateway may operate at any OSI layer, including the application layer — it is not restricted to Layer 3.",
      "The default gateway configured on a host is the address it sends off-subnet traffic to, and is normally a router interface.",
      "Common real gateways: VoIP gateway, email gateway, payment gateway, IoT gateway.",
      "Because it does protocol conversion, a gateway is typically slower per packet than a router."
    ],
    "traps": [
      "Default gateway is not a protocol-translating gateway. It is the router address a host uses to leave its own subnet — the name is historical.",
      "Gateway vs router — the exam answer is 'a router connects similar networks (IP to IP), a gateway connects dissimilar ones by translating'.",
      "A gateway is not the same as a bridge. A bridge joins two segments of the same network at Layer 2 without translating protocols.",
      "'Every gateway is a router' is false. The reverse loose usage — calling a home router a gateway — is what people actually do."
    ],
    "mnem": "Gateway = translate, Router = route.",
    "rel": [
      "Routers",
      "IP Addressing",
      "OSI Model"
    ]
