    "id": "c95",
    "paper": "TECH2",
    "unit": "II",
    "unitTitle": "Database Management System",
    "sub": "Data Models",
    "def": "A data model is a set of concepts for describing the structure of a database: the data, the relationships among it, the constraints on it and its meaning. Models are grouped by level of abstraction into high-level or conceptual models (the ER model), representational or implementation models (relational, network, hierarchical) and low-level physical models.",
    "exp": "Hierarchical model: data arranged as a tree, where each child record has exactly one parent. It supports one-to-many naturally but cannot express many-to-many directly. IBM's IMS is the standard example and it is the oldest of the three.\n\nNetwork model: data arranged as a graph of owner and member records, where a record may have several parents, so many-to-many is supported. It was standardised by the CODASYL DBTG committee.\n\nRelational model: proposed by E. F. Codd in 1970 and now dominant. Data is held in relations, that is tables of tuples (rows) and attributes (columns). Links between tables are made by matching values through foreign keys, not by physical pointers, which is exactly what makes it easier to reorganise than the older two. SQL implements this model.\n\nEntity-Relationship model: a conceptual design model drawn as a diagram and then converted into relational tables. It is a design tool, not something you can query.\n\nObject-oriented and object-relational models add objects, classes and inheritance for complex data. The NoSQL families, document, key-value, column-family and graph, are the modern additions, chosen where schema flexibility or horizontal scale matters more than strict relational structure.\n\nRelational terminology worth memorising: relation is the table, tuple the row, attribute the column, domain the set of permitted values for an attribute, degree the number of attributes and cardinality the number of tuples.",
    "facts": [
      "The relational model was proposed by E. F. Codd in 1970.",
      "Hierarchical model is a tree with one parent per child; the network model is a graph and allows multiple parents, so it supports many-to-many.",
      "Relation = table, tuple = row, attribute = column, degree = number of columns, cardinality = number of rows.",
      "Domain is the set of permitted values for an attribute.",
      "The ER model is a conceptual (high-level) model; relational, network and hierarchical are representational or implementation models.",
      "The network model was standardised by CODASYL."
    ],
    "traps": [
      "Degree versus cardinality. Degree counts columns, cardinality counts rows. These are swapped in options more often than any other pair here.",
      "Hierarchical versus network: only the network model allows a record to have more than one parent.",
      "The relational model connects tables by values (foreign keys); the hierarchical and network models connect records by physical pointers.",
      "The ER model is a design notation, not an implementation model. You convert it into relational tables before anything can be queried."
    ],
    "mnem": "",
    "rel": [
      "ER Modelling",
      "Relational Database Management System",
      "Database Architecture"
    ]
