    "id": "c115",
    "paper": "TECH2",
    "unit": "II",
    "unitTitle": "Database Management System",
    "sub": "PostgreSQL (Concept)",
    "def": "PostgreSQL, commonly called Postgres, is a free and open-source object-relational database management system developed by a worldwide community under a permissive licence rather than by a single vendor. It is fully relational, ACID-compliant, strongly standards-oriented in its SQL, and runs on Linux, Windows and macOS.",
    "exp": "'Object-relational' means it goes beyond plain tables while remaining relational: user-defined data types, table inheritance, arrays and a rich built-in type set, plus JSON storage so semi-structured documents can sit inside a relational table and still be queried. It is deliberately extensible — PostGIS, the geographic extension, turns it into a full spatial database, which is why it shows up in land-records, survey and mapping work.\n\nIts procedural language is PL/pgSQL, used for stored procedures, triggers and functions; other languages can be plugged in. The command-line client is psql and pgAdmin is the usual graphical tool. For concurrency it uses multi-version concurrency control, so a reader sees a consistent snapshot and readers do not block writers.\n\nThe commercial fact is that there is no licence to buy and no per-core cost, and no vendor can withdraw the software. That matters for government and public-sector deployments, where licence cost, procurement and long-term vendor lock-in are real constraints, and it is a large part of why Postgres is common in Indian e-governance stacks and in Linux-based web application deployments. Support is optional and bought from a company if wanted, rather than bundled into the product price. Conceptually it offers what any serious RDBMS offers — transactions, roles and privileges, backup and recovery, replication; the differences from a proprietary peer lie in dialect, tooling and licensing.",
    "facts": [
      "PostgreSQL is free and open-source, released under a permissive licence, and is maintained by a community rather than owned by one vendor.",
      "It is described as an object-relational DBMS: it supports user-defined types, table inheritance, arrays and JSON alongside ordinary relational tables.",
      "Its procedural language is PL/pgSQL; psql is the command-line client and pgAdmin the common GUI tool.",
      "It uses multi-version concurrency control (MVCC), so readers do not block writers.",
      "The default TCP port for PostgreSQL is 5432.",
      "PostGIS is the extension that adds geographic and spatial data support."
    ],
    "traps": [
      "Open source does not mean 'no cost at all' — you still pay for hardware, administration skills and, if you want it, commercial support. It means no licence fee and no vendor lock-in.",
      "PostgreSQL vs MySQL — both are open source, but they are separate products with different licences, dialects and governance. MySQL is under Oracle's ownership; PostgreSQL is community-governed.",
      "Storing JSON does not make PostgreSQL a NoSQL database. It remains relational and ACID-compliant; JSON is one column type among many.",
      "PL/pgSQL, PL/SQL and T-SQL are three different procedural dialects (PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQL Server). The similar names are the trap."
    ],
    "mnem": "",
    "rel": [
      "Microsoft SQL Server (Concept)",
      "Relational Database Management System",
      "Concurrency Control"
    ]
